WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.860
UC San Diego researchers showed a cellular enzyme can read an 8-letter genetic code, expanding

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:13.620
beyond life's standard four bases. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 174. Researchers

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:20.500
at UC San Diego demonstrated that RNA polymerase processes an expanded genetic alphabet containing

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.640
8 letters instead of the four used by all-known life. Detailed imaging showed the enzyme

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.740
handles the synthetic bases through mechanisms similar to those for natural nucleochides.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:38.120
The work advances efforts to create expanded genetic systems capable of new biological functions.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.360
The finding narrows the gap between natural and artificial genetic systems. Next steps include

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:49.820
testing whether the expanded alphabet can support functional proteins or other cellular processes.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.680
Now, here's something most people get wrong about why DNA uses exactly four bases. Most

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:02.180
people assume the four DNA bases are chemically fixed because any change would break replication

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:08.780
or transcription. In reality, the enzymes that read DNA evolved flexible active sites that

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:15.600
can accommodate variations when the geometry fits. Right now, inside every cell, RNA polymerase

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.180
grips each base pair through hydrogen bonds and shape complementarity, rather than recognizing

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:28.640
the exact atoms of A, T, C, or G. The UC San Diego imaging shows the enzyme uses the same

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.100
contacts for synthetic letters, provided the overall size and pairing pattern remain similar.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.380
One memorable detail is that the expanded system still achieves accurate readout despite doubling

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.840
the alphabet size. A practical takeaway is to watch for future experiments that test whether

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.780
cells can maintain the new letters across multiple generations without accumulating errors. From

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.120
the flexibility of core cellular machinery, we move to how gene regulation shapes complex

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:03.700
social behaviors and insects. A team from Johannes Gutenberg University Mines and Tel Aviv University

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:10.020
examined Categlifus Niger worker ants that either performed or avoided rescue of distressed

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.420
nest mates. Differences appeared in gene expression within the nervous system rather than in body

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:22.260
size, morphology or energy stores. The Journal of Experimental Biology reports the findings. The

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:28.600
results point to neural gene regulation as a driver of altruistic actions in social insects. From

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.440
neural gene control and ants, we turn to another system where electron interactions dictate unexpected

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:41.440
material properties. Researchers suppressed superconductivity in magic angle graphene by screening

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:47.920
electron interactions, clarifying the mechanism behind the effect. The experiment helps settle whether

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:54.440
the superconductivity arises from conventional or unconventional pairing. Results support models in

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.120
which electron correlations play a central role. The work was conducted on devices with tunable

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:06.000
screening environments. Understanding hidden molecular players in physics leads naturally to a new

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.880
tool for identifying unknown molecules in biology. More than 80% of small molecules detected in

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:20.020
typical biological samples currently lack structural matches in existing databases. A new AI method maps

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:26.540
these unidentified compounds produced by the human body and its microbiome. The tool targets molecules

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:33.000
that influence immunity and metabolism. It addresses a persistent bottleneck in metabolomics research.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:39.780
From identifying molecules we cannot yet name, we look at how living organisms respond when exposed

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:46.820
to synthetic materials they have never encountered. Kyushu University researchers fed expanded polystyrene

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:53.060
to warfroaches and observed altered expression of genes involved in chemical defense, DNA repair

00:03:53.060 --> 00:04:00.340
and digestion. Lifespans showed no significant reduction despite the diet. The gut microbiome remained

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:05.440
largely stable, though several rare microbes appeared only in polystyrene fed animals.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.100
The study was published in marine pollution bulletin. From biological responses to novel materials,

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:18.200
we examine how everyday materials can be engineered for new functions. Harvard researchers used

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.520
elastic yarns and industrial knitting to produce thick textiles that lock into multiple curved

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.420
configurations. Adding conductive yarn converted the shape shifting material into switches that control

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:35.780
lights, count steps and respond to movement. The textiles maintain stable states without continuous

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:41.620
power input. The work demonstrates how everyday knitting techniques can yield programmable functional

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:47.920
fabrics. From programmable textiles we shift to another advance in controlling light at small scales.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:54.400
Lund University engineers created light emitting diodes using thin, branched nano wires that generate

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.540
light at controlled locations within the structure. By directing emission away from lost prone regions,

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.200
the design improves the fraction of light that exits the device. The approach also targets lower

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:12.120
production costs compared with conventional LED fabrication. The study appears in nano research.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:19.480
MIT Lincoln Laboratory deployed commercial sensors during US Navy Operation Ice Camp to record sounds

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:25.300
from cracking sea ice, beluga whales and shipping vessels beneath the Arctic Ocean. A higher

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:31.660
fidelity geophone version returned in March 2026 to measure vibrations transmitted through sea ice.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.380
The data help characterize how sound and mechanical signals propagate in the under-ice environment.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:42.540
Researchers aim to improve through ice communication techniques using these observations.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:48.060
A study of preschool children found that short sessions of either digital or traditional games

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:53.440
improved performance on tasks requiring rapid responses. The same sessions reduced

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.460
performance on tasks needing sustained concentration and self-control. The results distinguish between

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:04.640
effects on different components of attention. Researchers tracked both digital and physical play

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:11.000
conditions. Researchers from Kanazawa University, the University of Tokyo and Horeba showed that

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.580
the starting arrangement of platinum on carbon particles strongly influences how polymer electrolyte

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:23.000
fuel cell inks disperse after ionomer addition. The initial particle state before mixing determines

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:29.600
later ink properties. The work appears in the chemical engineering journal. A three-year collaboration

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.180
between Florida State University and the Leon County Sheriff's Office tracked real-world outcomes

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:41.220
of de-escalation instruction. The study provides one of the most detailed field evaluations of whether

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:47.020
officers can learn and apply these techniques. Results indicate measurable improvements in trained

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:52.960
skills during encounters. The work focuses on practical implementation rather than classroom metrics

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:59.640
alone. Teams at the University of Leage and K.U. Loovin used experimental patrology to recreate

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:05.780
the planet's magma ocean crystallization and core formation. Multiple studies published in Earth

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:11.840
and planetary science letters, nature communications, and advances in geochemistry and cosmochemistry

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:18.440
map how graphite crust and metallic core developed. The work prepares context for Bappy-Colombo

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:24.880
mission data. Findings refine models of Mercury's earliest thermal evolution. A NIMS and

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:31.200
University of Tokyo team coded magnetic insulator powders with metal then centered them to create three

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.740
dimensional nano interfaces throughout a macroscopic material. The structure produced thermal

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:42.340
electric conversion driven by electron spins previously seen only in thin film interfaces.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:48.580
The composite demonstrates scalable versions of nanoscale spin effects. Researchers found that

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:53.600
wild birdies using supplemental food and water stations were three times more likely to acquire

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:59.700
disease-causing parasites than birds avoiding those resources. The study links artificial feeding

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:05.700
sites to higher transmission of parasites among farmland species. Data were collected across multiple

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:11.100
sites with varying station densities. The work highlights trade-offs in wildlife provisioning

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:16.620
practices. Genetic material recovered from medieval manuscripts reveal sequences of the sheep

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:23.380
pox virus, a pathogen that historically affected livestock. The parchedments act as biological time

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:29.920
capsules that may contain additional records of past animal disease outbreaks. Similar archives worldwide

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.720
could yield further pathogen genomes. The approach offers a new route to reconstruct historical

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:41.640
disease ecology. Next time keep an eye on results from the Beppy Colombo mission as they test the

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:48.240
new models of Mercury's early crust and core. That's Planetarian Daily for today. If you enjoyed this,

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:54.700
a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show. I'm Patrick

